(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609093400-dot-was-wrong-and-the-suite-was-told-to-look-away")
 (:adr/title "`.` was wrong, and the suite had been told to look away")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-09")
 (:adr/authority "owner instruction, 2026-09-09")
 (:adr/repos "kotoba-lang/pattern, kotoba-lang/cssom")
 (:adr/related "adr-2609093300-captures-and-the-end-of-the-regex-blocker, adr-2809041200, adr-2609092600-a-program-outgrew-the-document-and-the-corpus-hid-a-bug, kotoba-lang/pattern#3")
 (:adr/body "Continuing 2026-09-09, after captures. The next step was cssom's three named
gaps. Measuring the first one found something else first.

## The bug

`.` matched a line terminator in both matchers. JavaScript's does not.
Measured that day: `a.b` against \"a\\nb\" answered TRUE here and false there,
and so did `.` against \"\\n\" and `a.*b` against \"a\\nxb\".

## Why it survived

`vm_parity` was 270/270 and `pattern_parity` 252/252, both green for days,
and NEITHER COULD HAVE CAUGHT IT. Both headers listed this among \"two
differences that are deliberate and asserted rather than hidden\", and both
then stated that patterns containing `.` are \"compared only on inputs with no
newline\". `pattern_core`'s own docstring said `.` takes a newline \"which
differs from Clojure default and is asserted in the parity test rather than
left to be discovered\".

One of those two differences is real. This is a Pike VM: leftmost-longest,
where JavaScript backtracks. That is a property, it has a derivation, and the
suites are right to pin it.

The other was a line of code. Op 2 answered `true`. Nothing about a Pike VM
requires `.` to take a newline; it is simply what that line did. Written up as
a decision, it then DID WORK: it justified leaving newline inputs out of the
corpus, so the suite was blind by design and the blindness had a rationale
sitting next to it.

This is ADR-2809041200 exactly -- an implementation state promoted to a
property -- with the additional turn that the promotion was enforced by a test
corpus. The rule that ADR gives (\"その一文は、書き手が実装状態を性質に昇格させた
瞬間の痕跡である\") has a corollary worth stating: when a difference is written
up as deliberate, check what the write-up is being used to justify NOT
testing.

It is also the second time this corpus failed to reach a construct. The first
was patterns ending in `$` (adr-2609092600). Both times the suite was green,
both times the missing thing was an input rather than a check.

## What changed

`.` excludes LF, CR and the two Unicode separators, in `pattern-vm` and in
`pattern-core`. A negated class still matches them -- `[^x]` is not `.` -- in
both engines, and that is asserted.

`(?s)` turns it back on, and `(?m)` came with it. The header did not have to
widen: the nibble after \"K\" was a fold BIT and is now a bitfield (0 fold,
1 dotall, 2 multiline), so \"K0\" and \"K1\" still mean what they meant and every
program literal already compiled and committed still reads correctly. The same
reasoning as the capture slot count -- a format is a public surface even
inside one repo.

The emitter reads the flag LETTERS rather than matching \"(?i)\" as a
four-character string, so `(?is)`, `(?smi)` and the rest work in any order.
Leading only: a mid-pattern flag group is still refused, because modes live in
the header and would silently apply to the whole pattern. That refusal is an
implementation limit, not a design decision, and it is labelled as one.

`pattern-core` gets the `.` fix but not the flags: its program is a document
with a single `:fold` and no room for a mode field. That is a reason to use
`pattern-vm`, not a reason to leave the two matchers answering different
questions.

## Measured

  pattern-vm selfcheck    16/16
  pattern-emit selfcheck  17/17
  vm parity              320/320   was 270 -- the corpus now reaches `.`
  pattern parity         284/284   was 252 -- same
  emit parity            168/168   + 5 ahead of the .cljc oracle
  compile parity         157/157
  capture parity        1277/1277
  expressible            168/184   the inline-flags entry LEFT the ratchet

Against JavaScript directly: 11/11 on dot and dotall, 10/10 on the multiline
anchors including `(?m)^$` against \"a\\n\\nb\".

Discrimination: reverting the dot fix in both matchers now costs 17 failures
in `vm_parity` and 11 in `pattern_parity`. The identical code was invisible to
both suites that morning, and that contrast is the result -- not the fix.
Separately, making the multiline `^` ask about `pos` instead of `pos - 1`
reddens only `test-multiline-anchors`, the off-by-one its own comment names.

## A decision the emitter forced

The Kotoba emitter now compiles five leading-flag patterns that the `.cljc`
oracle still refuses, so `emit_parity` had a choice. Folded into \"both
refused\" they vanish. Failed, they are wrong -- there is nothing to disagree
with. They are counted under their own name, \"ahead of the oracle\", and
ratcheted at 5, so the category cannot become the place disagreements hide.
Teaching the oracle inline flags lowers it.

The `expressible` ratchet lost its `:group/inline-flags` entry the same day it
was written, by the OTHER direction of the ratchet: a gap that closes must
leave the table rather than sit there granting room nothing uses. Both
directions have now fired on real work.

## Where the goal stands

htmldom remains fully expressible. cssom's remaining gaps are two, not three:
non-greedy quantifiers (3 patterns) and lookaround (2).

⚠ Non-greedy is not obviously reachable, and this is the honest reading rather
than a plan. Under leftmost-longest, `*?` and `*` CANNOT differ -- POSIX has
no greediness, because the overall match is the longest regardless of any
operator. Supporting it means either giving the VM priority semantics
(leftmost-first, which changes every answer the parity suites currently pin)
or refusing it permanently and rewriting the three consumer patterns. That is
a decision, not an implementation task, and it should be taken deliberately
rather than discovered halfway through.")))
